,"kind":"house","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06535,52.94718]},"291623":{"y":1810,"f":"approx","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"c. 1810","h":8,"osm":"way/80946553","kind":"house","listEntry":1249344,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1249344","qid":"Q26541486","sum":"Hall Cottage is a house on Holme Lane. The building dates from about 1810, according to Historic England (via Wikipedia). It is Grade II listed.","sumSrc":{"title":"OpenStreetMap · Historic England","kind":"Written from the map's data"},"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06603,52.94681]},"291624":{"h":10,"osm":"way/80946416","kind":"stables","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06508,52.94695]},"291625":{"where":"Holme Lane","h":8,"osm":"way/80945942","kind":"house","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06602,52.94587]},"291626":{"h":10,"osm":"way/80947001","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06702,52.94777]},"291627":{"h":10,"osm":"way/80947762","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06612,52.94751]},"291628":{"h":10,"osm":"way/80947051","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06894,52.94752]},"291629":{"h":10,"osm":"way/80948447","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.0736,52.94608]},"291630":{"aka":["Church of St Edmund"],"y":1250,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"13th century","h":10,"osm":"way/54411843","listEntry":1249315,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1249315","qid":"Q7587789","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/St%20Edmunds%20Holme%20Pierrepont.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Edmund%27s_Church,_Holme_Pierrepont","cat":"St Edmund's Church, Holme Pierrepont","stories":[{"layer":"holme","text":"Henry Pierrepont, 1st Marquess of Dorchester, largely rebuilt the medieval church in 1666, and in 1878 T. C. Hine added its chancel. Its monuments remember the Pierreponts, from Sir Henry, who died in 1499, onwards, and the satirical poet John Oldham. It is listed Grade I.","source":{"title":"St Edmund's Church, Holme Pierrepont","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Edmund%27s_Church,_Holme_Pierrepont","kind":"Wikipedia"},"weblinks":[["T. C. Hine","note:Thomas Chambers Hine"]],"links":["Thomas Chambers Hine"],"when":"1666","whenSrc":{"title":"St Edmund's Church, Holme Pierrepont","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Edmund%27s_Church,_Holme_Pierrepont","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"St Edmund's, Holme Pierrepont"}],"sum":"St Edmund's Church, Holme Pierrepont is a parish church in the Church of England in Holme Pierrepont, Nottinghamshire. The church is Grade I listed by the Department for Digital, Culture, Media and Sport as a building of outstanding architectural or historic interest.","sumSrc":{"title":"St Edmund's Church, Holme Pierrepont","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Edmund%27s_Church%2C_Holme_Pierrepont"},"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06956,52.94681]},"291631":{"where":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","h":10,"osm":"way/257038465","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06926,52.94706]},"291632":{"y":1520,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"Early 16th century","h":8,"osm":"way/54411856","kind":"house","listEntry":1249330,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listing/the-list/list-entry/1249330","qid":"Q5883398","image":"http://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Holme%20Pierrpoint%20Hall%20%28listed%20grade%20I%29%2C%20Nottingham.jpg","archive":[{"file":"Neale(1818) p3.208 - Holme Pierrepoint, Nottinghamshire.jpg","year":1818,"author":"John Preston Neale","licence":"Public domain"}],"article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont_Hall","cat":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","stories":[{"layer":"holme","text":"The Pierreponts held Holme from about 1280. Around 1500 the hall was built, one of the few brick houses of its time in Nottinghamshire. The family became Earls and Dukes of Kingston, then Earls Manvers, and the hall is listed Grade I.","source":{"title":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"c. 1280","whenSrc":{"title":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","see":[["National Watersports Centre","p733"]]},{"layer":"holme","text":"Pierrepont is Norman French for \"stone bridge\". In 1257 Sir Henry Pierrepont married Annora de Manvers, heir to Holme, and the family stayed for almost seven centuries: Charles I made Sir Robert Pierrepont Earl of Kingston-upon-Hull in 1628, and George I made Evelyn Pierrepont a duke in 1715. The estate was broken up and sold in 1940.","source":{"title":"Holme Pierrepont","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont","kind":"Wikipedia"},"when":"1257","whenSrc":{"title":"Holme Pierrepont","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont","kind":"Wikipedia"},"nm":"The Pierreponts"}],"sum":"Holme Pierrepont Hall is a medieval manor house located in the village of Holme Pierrepont, near Nottingham, Nottinghamshire, England. It is a Grade I listed building, recognised for its exceptional architectural and historical significance. Built between approximately 1490 and 1500 by Sir William Pierrepont, the hall is one of the few brick-built houses in Nottinghamshire from the period and remains in private ownership.","sumSrc":{"title":"Holme Pierrepont Hall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Holme_Pierrepont_Hall"},"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06884,52.947]},"291633":{"h":8,"osm":"way/80947561","kind":"house","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.06896,52.94457]},"291634":{"where":"Holme Lane","h":10,"osm":"way/80948142","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.07203,52.94574]},"291635":{"y":1820,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipe